#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084318} :activity/id #uuid "66462250-9bf6-5b79-837b-72d46f60efbf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867 :topic/id #uuid "6cb8c050-5f50-5c96-a3dc-144c8d409165" :topic/math-academy-id 753} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084499 :tutorial/content "Consider the mapping diagram of the function $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/41/415b9ce71454f2aec9a2fc678c79f1d34fbb16468fbac50fc62b76283b9c209a.png)\n\nLet's now reverse the arrows, as follows:\n\n![](images/80/801b3eca4820b110c2145bd13fbf1faddbb0db83905869410cad1dc8a32f4e83.png)\n\nNow, we have a mapping diagram that \"reverses\" the action of the function $f$. The diagram itself represents a new function, which we call the **inverse of the function $f$,** and denote by $f^{-1}$.\n\nWe can evaluate the inverse function $f^{-1}$ at any point in the range of $f$. For example, to find the value of $f^{-1}(2)$, we notice that we have an arrow from ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right) to ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left). Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}({\\color{black}2}) = {\\color{black}0}\n$$\n\nIn the original function, $f$, that same arrow went from ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left) to ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right). So, we have that\n\n$$\nf({\\color{black}0})={\\color{black}2}\n$$\n\nand as a result, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(0)) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, applying the function $f$ and then \"reversing\" the action using $f^{-1}$ will get us back to $0$.\n\nIn general, this is true for every $x$ in the domain of the function $f$:\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "05fd6236-8b7b-59d7-bf60-5e7d9d2db65a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f836d465-3cfd-57b6-ab05-79619fabc9a6" :step/math-academy-id 41715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082904 :question/id #uuid "bca42c78-0735-587e-914b-7c9892f47d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-10986"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050633}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083878}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "2e3f4334-5c00-58e4-9032-fed944bab672" :step/math-academy-id 41716 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082905 :question/id #uuid "841640c2-1f50-5a13-b12b-bfe202e7621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10987"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083990}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"} :step/id #uuid "d19f2942-df0f-5896-adf0-8c5b3ca192b5" :step/math-academy-id 41719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084321}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084321 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082906 :question/id #uuid "200ba5c0-9ec6-56e9-8ce4-24cee169ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10988"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083879}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "26073589-e276-519e-b495-acab562e4cb3" :step/math-academy-id 41722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084322}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084322 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084500 :tutorial/content "Recall that the $f^{-1}$ \"reverses\" the action of $f$ so that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized using the mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9124176153ba77d905cf4cafad36d700f87ed5519a9f2e302b8a4dee6a6e1a51.png)\n\nSince $f^{-1}(f(x))$ is just the composition $(f^{-1} \\circ f)(x)$, we can now give the algebraic definition of the inverse.\n\nThe function $f^{-1}$ is called the **inverse of the function $f$** if\n\n$$\n(f^{-1} \\circ f)(x) = x\n$$\n\nfor each $x$ in the domain of $f$.\n\nIn other words, to show that two functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are inverses of each other, we need to check the following condition:\n\n$$\n(g\\circ f)(x) = x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f4a2e52b-5aee-58ed-bcc3-b236246d862c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6884 :tutorial/title "The Definition of the Inverse Function"} :step/id #uuid "3130ad01-93cf-56c2-a59d-52d2324277cf" :step/math-academy-id 41725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082907 :question/id #uuid "062748b1-c85a-5c2c-8d1f-f1006e73a880" :question/math-academy-id "e-10989"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234200}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "84556b63-2621-5c1b-84d0-55dbf5fff5d9" :step/math-academy-id 41726}] :activity/title "Introduction to Inverse Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083443}]}
